  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<ul><li><strong>Scientific name:</strong> Ameiurus serracanthus (Yerger &amp; Relyea, 1968)</li><li><strong>Common names:</strong> Spotted Bullhead; Hvidplettet Dværgmalle (Denmark)</li><li><strong>Synonym:</strong> Ictalurus serracanthus</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> 280 mm or 11\" SL</li><li><strong>Etymology:</strong> Ameiurus: \"curtailed\", refers to the lack of a deep notch in the caudal fin. The specific epithet serracanthus refers to the serrated pectoral spine.</li><li><strong>Type locality:</strong> Northern shore of Lake Talquin, 6.8 miles west-southwest of Ochlockonee River, Gadsden County, Florida, U.S.A.</li><li><strong>IUCN Red List:</strong> Least Concern (last assessed 2011).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>North America: Gulf Coastal Plain in Suwannee, St. Marks, Ochlockonee, Apalachicola and St. Andrews Bay drainages of northern Florida, southern Georgia and southeastern Alabama in the USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<ul><li>pH: 6.6 - 8.4</li><li>Temperature: 15.0-24.0°C (59-75.2°F)</li><li>Other parameters: Very tolerant generally, even towards brackish water.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>A sand substrate with stones and large piece of driftwood to hide under during the day is very desirable.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Omnivorous. Diet includes fish, aquatic invertebrates and some green matter.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Social with most anything they can not swallow, to be on the safe side do not house with anything less than half it's own size.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Females are much fuller bodied during breeding season. This occurs during early spring.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Not known in the aquarium. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Identification: One of the easier ictalurids to recognize. It has a relatively dark body with many small bold white spots which no other bullhead has. There is a dark oval blotch at the base of the dorsal fin. The upper jaw is longer than the lower jaw. Caudal fin and the relatively short rounded anal fin have a distinct narrow black margin. Not to be confused with the southern mottled form of Ameiurus nebulosus.</p><p>General remarks: A spectacular looking fish. A great catfish to keep for those looking for a smaller predatory fish. In the wild, it has a limited distribution and is not commonly found even in its own range.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1089",
      "html": "<p>Copeia 1968 (no. 2), pp 373, Fig. 4.</p>"
    }
  ],
